    19	# Design analysis
    20	# ---------------
    21	# concretization_and_trace:
    22	# - Preserved scaffold: black background, one fixed anchor containing yellow (4), red (2) boundary
    23	#   connectors, output-first generation, and red-on-red overlap as the only intended join.
    24	# - D1/2 effect in pixels: a scattered movable sprite may appear rotated or mirrored relative to its
    25	#   solved pose, so connector groups and bbox dimensions are no longer fixed by the input crop.
    26	# - D7/2 effect in pixels: the solved topology is a simple relay chain
    27	#   anchor -> piece1 -> piece2 -> ... -> pieceN. The anchor contributes one red pair, every internal
    28	#   piece has two red pairs, and the terminal piece has one red pair.
    29	# - Small trace:
    30	#   original task: after attaching piece A to the anchor, the next step may use any still-unpaired red
    31	#   on the whole growing object, and each piece is only translated.
    32	#   fused variant: after attaching piece A, only A's other connector pair is the legal next target; if
    33	#   piece B arrives rotated 90 degrees or mirrored, the solver must first pick the correct dihedral
    34	#   variant of B and only then translate that variant so one of B's red pairs exactly covers A's tail.
    35	#   The output uses the recovered orientation, not the scattered one.
    36	#
    37	# complexity_calibration:
    38	# - Pure chaining already reduces branching, so to keep the D1 change visible every movable piece is
    39	#   rendered with an asymmetric interior spur and is accepted only if it has several distinct dihedral
    40	#   variants. This makes the orientation mismatch legible to humans.
    41	# - Pure rotation/flip can explode the search space, so chaining limits the active attachment frontier to
    42	#   exactly one 2-cell red group. This keeps search manageable while still requiring orientation inference.
    43	# - Movable piece colors are sampled without repetition. That is not logically required by the rule, but it
    44	#   prevents accidental same-color overlap ambiguities under `_try_place` and keeps the visual parse clean.
    45	#
    46	# implicit_assumption_audit_of_original_main:
    47	# - `p["shape"]` and `p["reds"]` were treated as fixed. After D1/2 they depend on the chosen dihedral
    48	#   transform, so the solver must enumerate variant records with transformed bbox, red cells, and red groups.
    49	# - The original DFS expanded from "any count==1 red coordinate". After D7/2 that is too permissive: the
    50	#   state must remember the unique active tail group, otherwise the search would silently reintroduce tree
    51	#   branching.
    52	# - Original move deduplication by `(piece, top, left)` is insufficient once orientation changes matter.
    53	#   The fused solver deduplicates by `(piece, variant, top, left)`.
    54	# - `_try_place(cur, p["arr"], ...)` originally assumed the extracted sprite already matched the solved pose.
